{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "סמך לא תהיה קדשה ללא תסגיר עבד", :translit "samakh lo tihyeh kedesha le-lo tasgir eved", :gloss "adjoined 'there shall be no qedeshah' to 'you shall not surrender a slave'"} {:he "לא תהיה קדשה", :translit "lo tihyeh kedesha", :gloss "there shall be no qedeshah"} {:he "לא תסגיר עבד", :translit "lo tasgir eved", :gloss "you shall not surrender a slave"} {:he "בת ישראל הנשואה לעבד", :translit "bat yisrael ha-nesu'ah le-eved", :gloss "daughter of Israel married to a slave"} {:he "עבד", :translit "eved", :gloss "slave"} {:he "בנות ישראל", :translit "benot yisrael", :gloss "daughters of Israel"} {:he "בתך בגרה שחרר עבדך ותן לה", :translit "bitekh bagrah shaher avdekha ve-ten la", :gloss "your daughter has grown up, free your slave and give him to her"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Semikhut parshiyot: the verbal link between adjacent passages 'lo tihyeh kedesha' and 'lo tasgir eved' drives the interpretation that both address the scenario of a free Israelite woman and a Canaanite slave"} {:kind :other, :note "Additional semikhut linking 'eved' to 'benot yisrael' yields the practical halakhic directive to free a slave so he may marry an Israelite daughter"}], :frame {:text "Midrashic exegesis frequently derives law from the adjacencies (semikhut parshiyot) of biblical passages; R. Ishmael's thirteen hermeneutical principles include deriving laws from juxtaposition (Sifra, introduction). The specific reading here reads Deuteronomy 23:18–19 in light of the immediately preceding law of the fugitive slave in Deuteronomy 23:16–17, weaving them into a single scenario. The Sages understood 'qedeshah' in Deuteronomy 23:18 as referring to a woman designated for forbidden sexual union, including a woman who is stashed away for a slave (Rashi on Deuteronomy 23:18, citing Bemidbar Rabbah 10:9). The directive to free a Canaanite slave so that he may marry a Jewish woman reflects the principle that a slave who is freed attains the status of a Jew and may enter valid marriage (Yevamot 47b–48a; Gittin 40a–b).", :sources ["Sifra introduction (R. Ishmael's 13 principles)" "Rashi on Deuteronomy 23:18" "Bemidbar Rabbah 10:9" "Yevamot 47b" "Yevamot 48a" "Gittin 40a" "Gittin 40b"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Semikhut parshiyot: the verbal link between adjacent passages 'lo tihyeh kedesha' and 'lo tasgir eved' drives the interpretation that both address the scenario of a free Israelite woman and a Canaanite slave", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "Additional semikhut linking 'eved' to 'benot yisrael' yields the practical halakhic directive to free a slave so he may marry an Israelite daughter", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:38:45.263596457Z", :model "glm-5.2"}